Fix ECH writing past the end of the line, into the next one

ECH (CSI Ps X) erases Ps characters starting at the cursor. Tera Term clamps
Ps to the terminal WIDTH and then writes that many cells starting AT THE
CURSOR, so the write runs off the end of the line by exactly the cursor's
column:

  vtterm.c:CSEraseCharacter()
      CheckParamVal(Param[1], NumOfColumns);      /* clamp to width      */
      BuffEraseChars(Param[1]);                   /* -> XStart = CursorX */

  buffer.c:BuffEraseCharsInLine()
      memsetW(&(CodeLineW[XStart]), 0x20, ..., Count);

CodeBuffW is one contiguous allocation of NumOfColumns * lines cells, so the
overflow lands in the FOLLOWING line and silently rewrites its first CursorX
cells -- text, attributes and colours alike. On the last line of the buffer
there is no following line and the write goes past the end of the allocation.

The parameter is attacker-controlled: it arrives in the byte stream, and a
terminal reads that stream from the network. `printf '\033[999X'` with the
cursor at column 40 is a 40-cell out-of-bounds heap write.

Clamp in BuffEraseCharsInLine() rather than at the call site, so every caller
is covered and Count stays consistent with the redraw range computed from it
further down.

Reproduction, via the Sterna oracle (which runs vtterm.c + buffer.c headless).
Eleven characters, then erase to end of line -- row 4 is never written to:

    printf 'ECH line\033[999X' | oracle --cols 40 --rows 6 --attrs

    before:  4 |fffffffffff.............................|
    after:   4 |........................................|

The 'f' cells on row 4 are the eleven that overflowed from row 3.

Found by diffing Tera Term's own tests/bcetest.sh against a reimplementation.
Tera Term 5.7.0-dev (commit 827a35b05).

--- a/teraterm/teraterm/buffer.c
+++ b/teraterm/teraterm/buffer.c
@@ -1109,6 +1109,9 @@ void BuffEraseCharsInLine(int XStart, int Count)
 		CursorX = CursorXSave;
 	}

+	if (Count > NumOfColumns - XStart) {
+		Count = NumOfColumns - XStart;
+	}
 	NewLine(PageStart+CursorY);
 	memsetW(&(CodeLineW[XStart]),0x20, CurCharAttr.Fore, CurCharAttr.Back, AttrDefault, CurCharAttr.Attr2 & Attr2ColorMask, Count);

